1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,850
From the car park

2
00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:02,038
it is a pair of doors.

3
00:00:02,740 --> 00:00:03,951
Wide, painted, obvious.

4
00:00:04,780 --> 00:00:05,680
The way in.

5
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:07,960
One step inside there is a small

6
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:09,740
lobby, and at the far side

7
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:11,424
of that lobby there is one door.

8
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:13,880
On a February night in 2003,

9
00:00:14,620 --> 00:00:18,380
somewhere between four hundred and forty

10
00:00:18,380 --> 00:00:21,100
and four hundred and fifty-eight
people were in the building behind it.

11
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:21,800
They had about ninety seconds.

12
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:24,200
So why does a building have two

13
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:25,880
doors on the outside and one

14
00:00:25,880 --> 00:00:26,780
on the inside?

15
00:00:28,460 --> 00:00:29,723
Start with the building,

16
00:00:30,180 --> 00:00:31,654
because it is very ordinary,

17
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:32,958
and that is the point.

18
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:35,360
The Station stood at 211 Cowesett Avenue

19
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:36,939
in West Warwick, Rhode Island.

20
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:38,300
Single storey.

21
00:00:38,620 --> 00:00:39,520
Wood frame.

22
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:40,880
No sprinklers.

23
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:44,440
About four hundred and twelve

24
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:48,480
square metres — call it a large barn. Roadside
buildings like it exist in every state.

25
00:00:48,860 --> 00:00:50,228
That is not an accusation.

26
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:52,540
It is a description of the population

27
00:00:52,540 --> 00:00:53,840
this story belongs to.

28
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:55,740
Three exits were available

29
00:00:55,740 --> 00:00:56,845
to the people inside.

30
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:01,600
The front main entrance, on the north
side. A single door on the west,

31
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:03,460
near the platform the band used

32
00:01:03,460 --> 00:01:04,360
as a stage.

33
00:01:04,780 --> 00:01:07,574
And a single door on the east,
at the side of the main bar.

34
00:01:08,020 --> 00:01:09,860
There was a fourth door, in the kitchen.

35
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:12,476
It was not considered accessible to patrons.

36
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:14,540
So: three.

37
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:16,410
Now look closely at the first one,

38
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:17,840
because the entire film

39
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:18,998
is inside this detail.

40
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:20,700
The front main entrance

41
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:21,700
is a pair of doors.

42
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:24,420
From outside, from the car park,

43
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:26,420
from the road, it is plainly

44
00:01:26,420 --> 00:01:28,020
the way in and plainly wide.

45
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:31,194
Behind those doors is a vestibule —

46
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:33,980
a small lobby, the sort of thing

47
00:01:33,980 --> 00:01:35,340
a New England building

48
00:01:35,340 --> 00:01:37,200
has so that February does not come inside

49
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:38,674
every time somebody arrives.

50
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:39,920
And on the far side

51
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:41,815
of that vestibule there is one door.

52
00:01:42,380 --> 00:01:44,260
NIST states the consequence in a single

53
00:01:44,260 --> 00:01:46,540
line, and it is the most important

54
00:01:46,540 --> 00:01:47,751
sentence in the report.

55
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:49,900
The rate of egress from the main

56
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:51,860
entrance was limited by the single doorway

57
00:01:51,860 --> 00:01:53,787
inside the vestibule —

58
00:01:53,788 --> 00:01:58,000
not by the double doors visible
from the outside. Stay with that a moment,

59
00:01:58,000 --> 00:01:59,640
because it is not a defect

60
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:00,760
anyone would see.

61
00:02:01,460 --> 00:02:02,880
Walk in, and you go

62
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:03,900
through the wide doors first

63
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:05,268
and the narrow one second.

64
00:02:05,980 --> 00:02:07,822
Your experience of the entrance is:

65
00:02:08,040 --> 00:02:09,040
wide, then a small

66
00:02:09,040 --> 00:02:10,240
lobby, then through.

67
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:12,600
Nobody arriving at a club at nine

68
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:14,280
o'clock at night forms a view

69
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:15,859
about the throughput of a vestibule.

70
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:19,500
And the building looks,
from the road, exactly as capacious

71
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:20,658
as its widest opening.

72
00:02:21,440 --> 00:02:22,420
So why put a second,

73
00:02:22,420 --> 00:02:23,841
narrower door there at all?

74
00:02:24,440 --> 00:02:26,177
For the reason every cold-climate

75
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:27,400
building has one.

76
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:29,960
A vestibule is an air lock.

77
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:31,520
It stops the weather arriving

78
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:33,360
in the room every time somebody opens

79
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:35,600
a door, it stops the heat leaving,

80
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:37,700
and it gives a doorman somewhere

81
00:02:37,700 --> 00:02:39,800
to stand that is neither outside

82
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:41,680
in February nor inside the noise.

83
00:02:42,380 --> 00:02:44,012
It is a good piece of building.

84
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:45,460
It is in tens

85
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:46,565
of thousands of them.

86
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:48,760
And it is the narrowest thing

87
00:02:48,760 --> 00:02:49,660
on the route out.

88
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:51,260
Now the walls.

89
00:02:51,540 --> 00:02:54,913
The platform used as a stage
was lined with polyurethane foam —

90
00:02:54,914 --> 00:02:56,300
on portions of the walls,

91
00:02:56,300 --> 00:02:57,774
and on the ceiling above it.

92
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:01,880
Foam goes on the walls of a small music
venue for a reason that has nothing

93
00:03:01,880 --> 00:03:02,780
to do with fire.

94
00:03:03,320 --> 00:03:04,560
It is there for sound.

95
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:06,900
A room with hard parallel walls

96
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:08,760
and a low ceiling is unpleasant

97
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:10,240
to play in and unpleasant

98
00:03:10,240 --> 00:03:11,180
to stand in.

99
00:03:11,660 --> 00:03:13,186
Foam absorbs the reflections.

100
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:15,160
It is cheap, it is light,

101
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:16,800
and it goes up with adhesive

102
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:17,740
in an afternoon.

103
00:03:18,740 --> 00:03:20,161
Thousands of rooms have it.

104
00:03:20,440 --> 00:03:21,861
What matters is which foam.

105
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:23,960
After the fire, NIST bought foam

106
00:03:23,960 --> 00:03:25,680
and ran the test that nobody

107
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:26,785
had run in that building.

108
00:03:27,180 --> 00:03:29,500
They took a non-fire-retarded polyurethane

109
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:32,120
foam and set a pyrotechnic device beside

110
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:33,720
it, arranged as it

111
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:35,194
had been on the club's platform.

112
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:37,720
It ignited in less than fifteen seconds.

113
00:03:38,440 --> 00:03:41,410
Then they took a fire-retarded
polyurethane foam —

114
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:43,080
the same material class,

115
00:03:43,240 --> 00:03:44,924
the same purpose, the same look,

116
00:03:45,180 --> 00:03:46,767
bought off the shelf —

117
00:03:46,768 --> 00:03:48,399
and did exactly the same thing.

118
00:03:49,060 --> 00:03:50,376
It did not ignite at all.

119
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,540
And then they removed the foam entirely
and exposed the bare wood panelling

120
00:03:54,540 --> 00:03:55,540
to the same source.

121
00:03:56,020 --> 00:03:57,652
The wood did not ignite either.

122
00:03:58,240 --> 00:03:59,140
Same device.

123
00:03:59,540 --> 00:04:00,440
Same arrangement.

124
00:04:01,020 --> 00:04:01,920
Same room.

125
00:04:02,300 --> 00:04:04,000
Three outcomes, and only one of them

126
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:04,900
ends this way.

127
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:07,260
Now, what was actually on those walls.

128
00:04:07,780 --> 00:04:09,833
The foam was there as sound attenuation

129
00:04:10,114 --> 00:04:11,260
- on the rear wall,

130
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:12,520
on the platform wall,

131
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:13,720
and in the alcove

132
00:04:13,720 --> 00:04:14,773
where the drums sat.

133
00:04:15,420 --> 00:04:16,841
It is a packaging material.

134
00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:18,680
It is sold in sheets four feet

135
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:20,200
by eight, in grey,

136
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:23,240
flat or moulded
into the convoluted egg-crate

137
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:25,360
pattern that everybody recognises

138
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:26,700
from a rehearsal room.

139
00:04:27,260 --> 00:04:28,860
And the report states the thing

140
00:04:28,860 --> 00:04:31,300
that matters about the grade
normally sold for packing:

141
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:34,013
it typically contains no fire-retardant

142
00:04:34,360 --> 00:04:35,420
additives at all.

143
00:04:35,980 --> 00:04:37,138
Not a defective batch.

144
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:38,627
Not a counterfeit.

145
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:41,720
The ordinary product, doing the ordinary job

146
00:04:41,720 --> 00:04:42,560
it is sold for,

147
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:43,380
on a wall

148
00:04:43,380 --> 00:04:44,640
it was never sold for.

149
00:04:45,340 --> 00:04:47,820
The photographs of the interior
show it in detail.

150
00:04:48,260 --> 00:04:50,207
There are gaps where two sheets meet.

151
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:52,400
There are gaps between the foam

152
00:04:52,400 --> 00:04:54,320
and the wall at the outside corners

153
00:04:54,320 --> 00:04:55,220
of the alcove.

154
00:04:55,820 --> 00:04:56,900
And there is a detail

155
00:04:56,900 --> 00:04:58,700
in those photographs that tells you how

156
00:04:58,700 --> 00:05:00,560
long it had been there and how

157
00:05:00,560 --> 00:05:01,720
normal it had become.

158
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:04,395
Earlier pictures show the foam grey.

159
00:05:04,760 --> 00:05:06,657
Later pictures show it darker -

160
00:05:06,680 --> 00:05:07,960
the report says it

161
00:05:07,960 --> 00:05:09,539
may have been sprayed with black paint.

162
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:11,604
The surface has a glittery look,

163
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:13,973
possibly wet paint dusted with glitter.

164
00:05:14,620 --> 00:05:15,778
Somebody decorated it.

165
00:05:16,280 --> 00:05:17,596
Does that sound careless?

166
00:05:18,220 --> 00:05:19,120
It should not.

167
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:23,320
Painting the acoustic foam black is what
you do to a room that is going to be dark

168
00:05:23,320 --> 00:05:24,280
with a band in it.

169
00:05:24,940 --> 00:05:28,876
Grey packing foam looks like grey
packing foam. Black looks like a stage.

170
00:05:29,380 --> 00:05:31,180
Adding glitter is the same instinct

171
00:05:31,180 --> 00:05:32,080
one step further.

172
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:34,180
It is a decision about how

173
00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:36,680
the room feels, made by somebody thinking

174
00:05:36,680 --> 00:05:37,996
about how the room feels.

175
00:05:38,300 --> 00:05:40,140
Behind the foam was more foam:

176
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:42,880
rigid polystyrene thermal insulation,

177
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:45,180
laid between the wood studs.

178
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:46,340
Above all of it,

179
00:05:46,380 --> 00:05:49,360
the ceiling over the dance floor
and the platform had been raised

180
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:51,044
to about twelve and a half feet,

181
00:05:51,380 --> 00:05:52,480
built of gypsum board,

182
00:05:52,480 --> 00:05:54,300
with a lighting rack hung a hand's

183
00:05:54,300 --> 00:05:55,200
width below it.

184
00:05:55,720 --> 00:05:57,615
There was a second material as well,

185
00:05:57,760 --> 00:05:58,660
easy to overlook.

186
00:05:59,140 --> 00:06:01,360
A foam plastic thermal insulation

187
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:02,840
had been installed in the stud space

188
00:06:02,840 --> 00:06:04,480
of the drummer's alcove walls,

189
00:06:04,840 --> 00:06:06,720
with no fire-resistant barrier

190
00:06:06,720 --> 00:06:07,980
on the interior side.

191
00:06:09,160 --> 00:06:13,200
NIST measured all of it properly, in a cone
calorimeter, which does one thing:

192
00:06:13,720 --> 00:06:15,340
it exposes a small sample

193
00:06:15,340 --> 00:06:17,340
to a known heat and records how

194
00:06:17,340 --> 00:06:18,980
much energy comes back out.

195
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:21,080
At the standard exposure,

196
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:23,880
the non-flame-retarded foam peaked at around

197
00:06:23,880 --> 00:06:26,267
six hundred kilowatts per square metre.

198
00:06:26,440 --> 00:06:29,460
The flame-retarded foam peaked at four

199
00:06:29,461 --> 00:06:32,128
hundred and fifty-three. The ceiling
tile did not ignite at all.

200
00:06:32,340 --> 00:06:34,240
The wood panelling took forty-one seconds

201
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:35,398
to sustained ignition.

202
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:37,827
And one number from the night itself,

203
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:40,340
taken off the video frame by frame.

204
00:06:41,040 --> 00:06:42,780
The foam at the vertical corners

205
00:06:42,780 --> 00:06:45,640
of the drummer's alcove
ignited eight seconds

206
00:06:45,640 --> 00:06:48,000
after the pyrotechnic display was initiated.

207
00:06:48,760 --> 00:06:49,660
Eight seconds.

208
00:06:50,060 --> 00:06:53,140
One more thing about the testing,
and this programme is going to say

209
00:06:53,140 --> 00:06:54,880
it because leaving it out would make

210
00:06:54,880 --> 00:06:55,760
a cleaner story

211
00:06:55,760 --> 00:06:57,000
and a less true one.

212
00:06:57,540 --> 00:07:01,480
In another bench arrangement, NIST
aimed a device's sparks at foam

213
00:07:01,480 --> 00:07:03,560
with a deliberate gap and lip built

214
00:07:03,560 --> 00:07:06,180
in, specifically to catch hot sparks

215
00:07:06,180 --> 00:07:07,759
and make ignition more likely.

216
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:09,480
No ignition was observed.

217
00:07:10,340 --> 00:07:12,420
The sparks pitted the foam - small

218
00:07:12,420 --> 00:07:14,180
melted craters - and the process

219
00:07:14,180 --> 00:07:15,080
did not spread.

220
00:07:15,700 --> 00:07:16,760
So the honest statement

221
00:07:16,760 --> 00:07:18,280
is not that a device near

222
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:20,140
this foam always sets it alight.

223
00:07:20,780 --> 00:07:22,340
It is that the same device,

224
00:07:22,480 --> 00:07:24,440
the same foam and a slightly different

225
00:07:24,440 --> 00:07:26,220
geometry can give you a wall

226
00:07:26,220 --> 00:07:27,940
of pits or a wall on fire,

227
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:30,220
and nobody standing in the room

228
00:07:30,220 --> 00:07:31,160
can tell you in advance

229
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:32,320
which one they have.

230
00:07:33,060 --> 00:07:34,400
One more fact about the fuel,

231
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:36,160
and it changes what kind

232
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:37,423
of building this became.

233
00:07:38,180 --> 00:07:42,211
The wood structure and panelling contained
over ninety-five percent of the fuel load.

234
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:44,330
Once most of the foam was consumed,

235
00:07:44,360 --> 00:07:45,920
this stopped being a foam fire

236
00:07:45,920 --> 00:07:47,440
and became an ordinary

237
00:07:47,440 --> 00:07:48,756
wood-frame building fire.

238
00:07:49,440 --> 00:07:50,808
The foam was not the fire.

239
00:07:51,260 --> 00:07:52,628
The foam was the ignition.

240
00:07:54,420 --> 00:07:55,880
Before the numbers, what

241
00:07:55,880 --> 00:07:56,827
the number is for.

242
00:07:57,300 --> 00:07:59,210
An occupant limit is not a fire-safety

243
00:07:59,240 --> 00:08:01,540
decoration and it is not about comfort.

244
00:08:02,420 --> 00:08:04,262
It is arithmetic tied to the exits.

245
00:08:04,960 --> 00:08:08,660
You take the width of the ways
out, you take how fast people move

246
00:08:08,660 --> 00:08:10,397
through an opening of that width,

247
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:14,420
you take how long the building will stay
survivable, and you work backwards

248
00:08:14,420 --> 00:08:16,157
to how many people may be inside.

249
00:08:16,620 --> 00:08:18,260
That is the whole calculation.

250
00:08:19,060 --> 00:08:21,020
The limit is a statement about the doors.

251
00:08:21,580 --> 00:08:24,520
Which means that if the doors
do not change, the number cannot

252
00:08:24,520 --> 00:08:25,731
honestly change either.

253
00:08:26,140 --> 00:08:27,087
Now the paperwork.

254
00:08:27,900 --> 00:08:31,080
In 1981, an application
under the fire safety code —

255
00:08:31,380 --> 00:08:32,540
to leave the boiler room

256
00:08:32,540 --> 00:08:33,893
not fully enclosed —

257
00:08:34,440 --> 00:08:37,200
identified an occupant load of two hundred

258
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:39,658
and twenty-five for the building
as it was then being used.

259
00:08:39,620 --> 00:08:41,640
In a memorandum dated the thirtieth

260
00:08:41,640 --> 00:08:44,660
of December, 1999, the West Warwick Fire

261
00:08:44,660 --> 00:08:46,620
Department identified the occupant load

262
00:08:46,620 --> 00:08:48,607
as two hundred and fifty-three.

263
00:08:48,700 --> 00:08:51,020
And that same memorandum
granted an allowance

264
00:08:51,020 --> 00:08:54,660
to increase the occupancy to three hundred

265
00:08:55,560 --> 00:08:58,720
and seventeen — by removing
tables and chairs from three

266
00:08:58,720 --> 00:09:02,553
lounge areas and providing standing room only
in those areas. Read that mechanism carefully,

267
00:09:01,800 --> 00:09:03,260
because it is reasonable

268
00:09:03,260 --> 00:09:04,780
and it is exactly how

269
00:09:04,780 --> 00:09:05,680
the number moved.

270
00:09:06,440 --> 00:09:08,282
Tables and chairs take floor space.

271
00:09:08,560 --> 00:09:10,349
Take them out and more people fit.

272
00:09:10,900 --> 00:09:15,420
If the constraint you are thinking about is floor
area, then removing furniture genuinely

273
00:09:15,420 --> 00:09:16,940
does let you hold more people,

274
00:09:16,940 --> 00:09:18,420
and raising the number

275
00:09:18,420 --> 00:09:19,578
is the correct answer.

276
00:09:19,880 --> 00:09:21,340
But the constraint that kills

277
00:09:21,340 --> 00:09:22,240
is not the floor.

278
00:09:22,860 --> 00:09:24,560
It is the door.

279
00:09:24,560 --> 00:09:26,018
And nobody moved the door.

280
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:28,600
Against those figures, NIST offers the one

281
00:09:28,600 --> 00:09:29,500
from the codes:

282
00:09:30,200 --> 00:09:32,040
under the 2003 model codes,

283
00:09:32,420 --> 00:09:36,300
the occupant limit for a building like
The Station would have been around four

284
00:09:36,313 --> 00:09:37,313
hundred and twenty.

285
00:09:37,380 --> 00:09:40,280
And the number of people reported
to have been inside on the night

286
00:09:40,280 --> 00:09:43,071
was between four hundred and forty

287
00:09:43,072 --> 00:09:45,920
and four hundred and fifty-eight.
Where do those come from?

288
00:09:46,400 --> 00:09:48,740
News media reports, and an attribution

289
00:09:48,740 --> 00:09:51,498
to the state Attorney General's
office for the higher figure.

290
00:09:51,920 --> 00:09:55,300
This programme is going to be careful
here, because it is the kind

291
00:09:55,300 --> 00:09:57,142
of number that films state as fact.

292
00:09:58,020 --> 00:09:59,441
NIST itself tried to count.

293
00:10:00,320 --> 00:10:03,487
Investigators used a television crew's
video shot inside the club that night

294
00:10:03,260 --> 00:10:05,660
and counted the people visible in it.

295
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:07,920
Across four still frames as the camera

296
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:11,180
pans, one hundred and forty-four occupants

297
00:10:11,181 --> 00:10:13,000
can be seen. And NIST says plainly

298
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:14,480
that it is not clear at what

299
00:10:14,480 --> 00:10:16,120
point in the evening that sequence

300
00:10:16,120 --> 00:10:18,480
was recorded, so it cannot be scaled

301
00:10:18,480 --> 00:10:19,380
into a total.

302
00:10:19,710 --> 00:10:21,620
So the honest statement is this.

303
00:10:22,220 --> 00:10:24,400
The paperwork said two twenty-five,

304
00:10:25,680 --> 00:10:28,847
then two fifty-three, then three
seventeen. The codes of the year said

305
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:30,425
about four hundred and twenty.

306
00:10:30,480 --> 00:10:32,300
And the reported figure for that night

307
00:10:32,300 --> 00:10:34,876
was four hundred and forty to four

308
00:10:34,877 --> 00:10:37,760
hundred and fifty-eight. Every one
of those numbers was calculated

309
00:10:37,760 --> 00:10:39,380
for a building whose front exit

310
00:10:39,380 --> 00:10:40,340
was one door wide.

311
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:42,526
So who is supposed to notice?

312
00:10:42,840 --> 00:10:44,629
That is not a rhetorical question.

313
00:10:45,060 --> 00:10:46,060
There is an answer,

314
00:10:46,060 --> 00:10:47,460
and it has a job title.

315
00:10:47,980 --> 00:10:49,400
A code is only a document.

316
00:10:50,300 --> 00:10:52,120
What converts a document into a building

317
00:10:52,120 --> 00:10:54,400
is an inspection regime - somebody

318
00:10:54,400 --> 00:10:55,453
with a tape measure,

319
00:10:55,800 --> 00:10:57,420
a floor plan and the authority

320
00:10:57,420 --> 00:10:58,320
to say no.

321
00:10:58,980 --> 00:11:01,280
NIST's first recommendation is entirely

322
00:11:01,280 --> 00:11:03,520
about that machinery, and it reads like

323
00:11:03,520 --> 00:11:05,841
a list of the places
the machinery goes missing.

324
00:11:06,300 --> 00:11:08,280
It asks jurisdictions to adopt a model

325
00:11:08,280 --> 00:11:09,596
code and keep it updated.

326
00:11:09,980 --> 00:11:12,060
Then it asks for inspection and enforcement

327
00:11:12,060 --> 00:11:14,220
programmes that cover, specifically:

328
00:11:14,220 --> 00:11:17,560
documentation of building
permits and alterations;

329
00:11:18,720 --> 00:11:20,310
inspection of the means of egress,

330
00:11:20,340 --> 00:11:22,260
and the record keeping that goes with it;

331
00:11:22,660 --> 00:11:26,480
the frequency and the rigour of inspections,
including follow-up and auditing;

332
00:11:26,860 --> 00:11:29,720
and clear guidance on what
an inspector can actually

333
00:11:29,720 --> 00:11:31,141
do when they find a deviation.

334
00:11:31,580 --> 00:11:32,720
And then a plainer item

335
00:11:32,720 --> 00:11:33,600
than any of those:

336
00:11:33,600 --> 00:11:36,380
that enough inspectors and plan examiners

337
00:11:36,380 --> 00:11:38,320
are on the staff to do the job,

338
00:11:38,400 --> 00:11:40,663
and that they are professionally qualified.

339
00:11:41,020 --> 00:11:43,100
Enough people, properly trained,

340
00:11:43,100 --> 00:11:44,000
to go and look.

341
00:11:44,560 --> 00:11:46,900
NIST also lists, without adjectives,

342
00:11:46,900 --> 00:11:49,400
the ordinary behaviours that quietly switch

343
00:11:49,400 --> 00:11:50,700
a protective system off:

344
00:11:51,320 --> 00:11:53,445
installing decorations
that exceed the flame-spread

345
00:11:53,460 --> 00:11:56,260
provisions, exposing
a building to strong ignition

346
00:11:56,260 --> 00:11:59,360
sources, exceeding
the posted occupancy limits,

347
00:11:59,980 --> 00:12:01,440
temporarily blocking an exit,

348
00:12:01,440 --> 00:12:03,598
and disabling sprinklers for maintenance.

349
00:12:04,240 --> 00:12:05,714
Read that list again slowly.

350
00:12:06,200 --> 00:12:07,440
Every item on it is a thing

351
00:12:07,440 --> 00:12:10,020
somebody does on an ordinary
Tuesday afternoon,

352
00:12:10,420 --> 00:12:11,760
for an ordinary reason,

353
00:12:11,760 --> 00:12:12,660
in a building

354
00:12:12,660 --> 00:12:14,240
that is not on fire

355
00:12:14,240 --> 00:12:15,608
and will probably never be on fire.

356
00:12:16,320 --> 00:12:17,636
None of them is sabotage.

357
00:12:17,980 --> 00:12:19,960
Each is a small local decision

358
00:12:19,960 --> 00:12:21,720
that makes the room work better

359
00:12:21,720 --> 00:12:23,299
for the thing the room is for.

360
00:12:25,220 --> 00:12:26,280
Here is the clock.

361
00:12:27,100 --> 00:12:30,077
Every time on it is measured
from the moment the foam ignited.

362
00:12:30,540 --> 00:12:31,593
Twenty-four seconds.

363
00:12:32,060 --> 00:12:34,200
The first patrons recognise the danger.

364
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:35,900
Thirty seconds.

365
00:12:36,380 --> 00:12:38,960
The bulk of the crowd begins
to move, around the time

366
00:12:38,960 --> 00:12:40,171
the band stops playing.

367
00:12:40,500 --> 00:12:42,840
Six seconds between the first
person understanding

368
00:12:42,840 --> 00:12:44,682
and most of the room understanding.

369
00:12:45,480 --> 00:12:46,380
That is fast.

370
00:12:46,860 --> 00:12:50,126
Whatever else is true about that night,
the recognition was not slow.

371
00:12:50,900 --> 00:12:52,216
A little over one minute.

372
00:12:52,580 --> 00:12:54,580
Smoke is visible in the exit doorways.

373
00:12:55,240 --> 00:12:56,187
One minute eleven.

374
00:12:56,820 --> 00:13:00,865
People are leaving the building
in a steady stream. Note what that means.

375
00:13:01,260 --> 00:13:02,628
The evacuation is working.

376
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:04,340
There is a stream.

377
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:05,900
It is moving.

378
00:13:06,500 --> 00:13:07,763
About one minute thirty.

379
00:13:08,140 --> 00:13:10,420
The front exit becomes blocked with people.

380
00:13:11,200 --> 00:13:12,100
And on the video,

381
00:13:12,140 --> 00:13:14,100
occupants can be seen breaking windows

382
00:13:14,100 --> 00:13:15,660
and escaping from the pool room

383
00:13:15,660 --> 00:13:16,760
and the sun room.

384
00:13:17,500 --> 00:13:18,605
One minute forty-two.

385
00:13:19,260 --> 00:13:20,700
The crush at the front exit

386
00:13:20,700 --> 00:13:21,753
is on the recording.

387
00:13:22,520 --> 00:13:23,780
Now the part of the clock

388
00:13:23,780 --> 00:13:25,096
that is not on the video,

389
00:13:25,380 --> 00:13:26,906
because it is inside the air.

390
00:13:27,600 --> 00:13:29,840
NIST's simulation of the fire puts

391
00:13:29,840 --> 00:13:31,400
the dance floor and the areas next

392
00:13:31,400 --> 00:13:33,740
to it at untenable
temperatures within ninety

393
00:13:33,740 --> 00:13:34,793
seconds of ignition.

394
00:13:35,320 --> 00:13:37,080
The sun room and dart room reach

395
00:13:37,080 --> 00:13:39,133
the same state about ten seconds later.

396
00:13:39,820 --> 00:13:41,241
Untenable has a definition,

397
00:13:41,400 --> 00:13:43,380
and it is not a figure of speech.

398
00:13:43,680 --> 00:13:46,780
It is the point at which the temperature
passes a hundred

399
00:13:46,780 --> 00:13:48,460
and twenty degrees Celsius,

400
00:13:48,460 --> 00:13:51,120
or the heat flux passes a threshold,

401
00:13:51,120 --> 00:13:53,110
or the oxygen falls below twelve percent.

402
00:13:53,140 --> 00:13:56,358
And in the sun room beside the entranceway —

403
00:13:56,359 --> 00:14:00,028
a place where many
of the dead were later found —

404
00:14:00,029 --> 00:14:03,580
the simulation shows the temperature going
from ordinary room conditions to around

405
00:14:03,580 --> 00:14:05,500
a thousand degrees in ten seconds.

406
00:14:06,360 --> 00:14:10,660
NIST puts the human consequence of that in one
sentence, and it does not soften it.

407
00:14:11,220 --> 00:14:15,680
Comparing its own experiment with the full
nightclub, the report says the conditions around

408
00:14:15,680 --> 00:14:17,600
the platform, the dance floor,

409
00:14:18,100 --> 00:14:19,620
the sun room and the dart room

410
00:14:19,620 --> 00:14:22,200
would have led to severe incapacitation

411
00:14:22,200 --> 00:14:24,060
or death within about a minute

412
00:14:24,060 --> 00:14:27,113
and a half of ignition
for anyone remaining standing —

413
00:14:27,520 --> 00:14:28,880
and not much longer even

414
00:14:28,880 --> 00:14:30,406
for those close to the floor.

415
00:14:30,760 --> 00:14:32,840
So how long did anybody actually have?

416
00:14:33,740 --> 00:14:35,340
Ninety seconds, from a fire nobody

417
00:14:35,340 --> 00:14:36,840
in the room knew

418
00:14:36,840 --> 00:14:38,051
about until twenty-four seconds in.

419
00:14:38,460 --> 00:14:39,960
Which leaves about a minute.

420
00:14:40,620 --> 00:14:46,655
One minute, from the moment the room understood,
to the moment the room stopped being survivable —

421
00:14:46,656 --> 00:14:51,340
for four hundred and forty to four

422
00:14:51,340 --> 00:14:54,160
hundred and fifty-eight people, most
of whom were heading for one door.

423
00:14:53,260 --> 00:14:54,420
So set the two clocks

424
00:14:54,420 --> 00:14:55,367
beside each other.

425
00:14:55,900 --> 00:14:58,895
The crush at the front door
is on video at one minute forty-two.

426
00:14:59,260 --> 00:15:00,440
The air in that part

427
00:15:00,440 --> 00:15:03,340
of the building stopped
being survivable at ninety seconds.

428
00:15:04,120 --> 00:15:07,790
The blockage and the deadline
are the same twelve seconds apart. Now:

429
00:15:07,820 --> 00:15:09,031
where did everybody go?

430
00:15:09,720 --> 00:15:11,840
Between fifty-six and sixty-six percent

431
00:15:11,840 --> 00:15:13,780
of the occupants appear to have attempted

432
00:15:13,780 --> 00:15:15,740
to leave through the single main entrance

433
00:15:15,740 --> 00:15:16,640
at the front.

434
00:15:17,080 --> 00:15:18,291
Many were unsuccessful.

435
00:15:18,920 --> 00:15:20,400
Between a half and two thirds

436
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:21,780
of everyone in that building,

437
00:15:21,780 --> 00:15:22,720
at one door.

438
00:15:23,320 --> 00:15:25,162
That is not a failure of judgement.

439
00:15:25,320 --> 00:15:27,840
It is the most ordinary thing people

440
00:15:27,840 --> 00:15:28,964
do in a building.

441
00:15:29,280 --> 00:15:30,859
You leave the way you came in.

442
00:15:30,920 --> 00:15:31,973
You know that route.

443
00:15:32,600 --> 00:15:34,300
You walked it.

444
00:15:34,900 --> 00:15:36,410
In a room you have never studied,

445
00:15:36,440 --> 00:15:38,780
filling with smoke, in the dark,

446
00:15:39,100 --> 00:15:39,920
the way you came

447
00:15:39,920 --> 00:15:40,920
in is the only route

448
00:15:40,920 --> 00:15:42,078
your body already has.

449
00:15:42,700 --> 00:15:43,911
The side doors existed.

450
00:15:44,620 --> 00:15:45,778
The report lists them.

451
00:15:46,360 --> 00:15:49,160
But knowing that a door exists
and having walked through a door

452
00:15:49,160 --> 00:15:51,002
are not the same kind of knowledge,

453
00:15:51,320 --> 00:15:54,076
and only one of them is available
to you at ninety seconds.

454
00:15:54,600 --> 00:15:56,380
And here the report is more precise

455
00:15:56,380 --> 00:15:57,540
than the summaries of it,

456
00:15:57,540 --> 00:15:59,020
in a way that changes

457
00:15:59,020 --> 00:16:00,125
the picture entirely.

458
00:16:00,820 --> 00:16:02,020
The crush did not happen

459
00:16:02,020 --> 00:16:03,125
at the outside doors.

460
00:16:03,660 --> 00:16:05,540
It happened inside the vestibule.

461
00:16:06,240 --> 00:16:07,960
That small lobby - the one

462
00:16:07,960 --> 00:16:09,802
that exists so the winter stays

463
00:16:10,105 --> 00:16:11,579
out - is where the flow stopped.

464
00:16:11,960 --> 00:16:14,120
The report says the crush there almost

465
00:16:14,120 --> 00:16:16,280
entirely disrupted the flow through the main

466
00:16:16,280 --> 00:16:18,660
exit, and that many people became stuck

467
00:16:18,660 --> 00:16:19,880
in the prone position

468
00:16:19,880 --> 00:16:21,480
in the exterior double doors.

469
00:16:22,060 --> 00:16:22,960
Prone.

470
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:26,040
In the wide doors.

471
00:16:26,780 --> 00:16:28,420
The wide doors were not the problem

472
00:16:28,420 --> 00:16:29,560
and the wide doors

473
00:16:29,560 --> 00:16:30,560
are where it ended,

474
00:16:30,700 --> 00:16:32,820
because a single doorway upstream of them

475
00:16:32,820 --> 00:16:35,100
had already decided how many bodies per

476
00:16:35,100 --> 00:16:36,153
second could arrive.

477
00:16:36,600 --> 00:16:37,811
Once the front blocked,

478
00:16:38,280 --> 00:16:42,340
the windows of the main bar room and the sun
room became the secondary routes of escape.

479
00:16:42,940 --> 00:16:45,256
And the windows did something extraordinary.

480
00:16:45,580 --> 00:16:47,200
They accounted for over one third

481
00:16:47,200 --> 00:16:48,779
of the successful evacuations.

482
00:16:49,260 --> 00:16:50,700
More than a third of everyone

483
00:16:50,700 --> 00:16:52,220
who got out of that building

484
00:16:52,220 --> 00:16:53,120
did not use a door.

485
00:16:53,760 --> 00:16:55,076
They went through a wall.

486
00:16:55,600 --> 00:16:57,500
Think about what that number is made of.

487
00:16:58,060 --> 00:16:59,840
Somebody has to decide that the window

488
00:16:59,840 --> 00:17:00,740
is the way.

489
00:17:01,300 --> 00:17:02,480
Somebody has to break it,

490
00:17:02,480 --> 00:17:03,600
in a room where the air

491
00:17:03,600 --> 00:17:04,500
is already going.

492
00:17:04,940 --> 00:17:06,420
Somebody has to be near enough

493
00:17:06,420 --> 00:17:08,000
to a wall rather than caught

494
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:09,316
in the middle of a floor.

495
00:17:09,640 --> 00:17:11,380
And then somebody outside

496
00:17:11,380 --> 00:17:12,280
has to reach back in.

497
00:17:12,920 --> 00:17:14,940
The video shows occupants still being helped

498
00:17:14,940 --> 00:17:18,160
out through the main bar windows
at four minutes eight seconds.

499
00:17:18,850 --> 00:17:20,218
None of that is in a plan.

500
00:17:20,820 --> 00:17:22,188
All of it is in the count.

501
00:17:22,640 --> 00:17:23,745
Nobody designed that.

502
00:17:24,240 --> 00:17:25,240
Nobody drilled it.

503
00:17:25,700 --> 00:17:27,020
It is not on any plan.

504
00:17:27,720 --> 00:17:29,060
It happened because a window

505
00:17:29,060 --> 00:17:29,840
is a way

506
00:17:29,840 --> 00:17:31,440
out if you are willing to break it,

507
00:17:31,440 --> 00:17:33,700
and several hundred people worked

508
00:17:33,700 --> 00:17:35,226
that out at the same time in the dark.

509
00:17:35,580 --> 00:17:37,420
The last recorded escape from inside

510
00:17:37,420 --> 00:17:39,300
the main bar, through a window,

511
00:17:39,300 --> 00:17:41,060
is at four minutes eight seconds.

512
00:17:41,860 --> 00:17:45,460
And people stuck in the front entrance
are seen escaping as late as five

513
00:17:45,460 --> 00:17:48,200
and a half minutes into the fire —

514
00:17:48,201 --> 00:17:52,360
just before the fire department ran
its first hose line. In under five minutes,

515
00:17:52,500 --> 00:17:54,580
flames had come through part of the roof.

516
00:17:56,560 --> 00:17:58,560
The value of a technical investigation

517
00:17:58,620 --> 00:17:59,940
is that it can run the night

518
00:17:59,940 --> 00:18:01,800
again with one thing changed.

519
00:18:02,680 --> 00:18:03,627
NIST ran it twice.

520
00:18:04,260 --> 00:18:05,681
The first change was water.

521
00:18:06,040 --> 00:18:07,777
In the laboratory reconstruction,

522
00:18:08,420 --> 00:18:09,500
the club was rebuilt

523
00:18:09,500 --> 00:18:11,166
and burned twice —

524
00:18:11,167 --> 00:18:13,600
once without sprinklers and once with them.

525
00:18:14,080 --> 00:18:16,120
In the unsprinklered version,

526
00:18:16,780 --> 00:18:21,160
investigators fitted sprinkler heads
that had no water supply, purely to measure

527
00:18:21,160 --> 00:18:22,739
when they would have gone off.

528
00:18:22,920 --> 00:18:27,164
The first one above the platform would have activated
thirty-three seconds after ignition.

529
00:18:27,720 --> 00:18:28,825
Thirty-three seconds.

530
00:18:29,200 --> 00:18:31,120
Nine seconds after the first person

531
00:18:31,120 --> 00:18:33,225
in the room realised anything was wrong.

532
00:18:33,840 --> 00:18:35,472
The second change was the door.

533
00:18:36,300 --> 00:18:38,560
NIST modelled the evacuation
with the entrance

534
00:18:38,560 --> 00:18:40,620
doorway made twice as wide,

535
00:18:40,620 --> 00:18:42,520
and reports the result plainly:

536
00:18:43,160 --> 00:18:45,040
doubling the entrance door width brought

537
00:18:45,040 --> 00:18:49,160
the number of people still inside
the building at ninety seconds back down

538
00:18:49,160 --> 00:18:51,160
to the range of the baseline scenario.

539
00:18:51,600 --> 00:18:54,900
And there is a detail attached
to that calculation which matters.

540
00:18:55,560 --> 00:18:59,020
The crush had not occurred before ninety
seconds, and the front door remained open

541
00:18:59,020 --> 00:19:00,862
through those first ninety seconds.

542
00:19:01,180 --> 00:19:01,920
So the model

543
00:19:01,920 --> 00:19:03,394
is not describing a miracle.

544
00:19:03,760 --> 00:19:05,020
It is describing width.

545
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:07,560
There is a third thing the report

546
00:19:07,560 --> 00:19:09,297
settles, and it closes a question

547
00:19:09,820 --> 00:19:11,978
this kind of film usually leaves hanging.

548
00:19:12,330 --> 00:19:13,751
Where was the fire brigade?

549
00:19:14,460 --> 00:19:16,401
They were four hundred metres away.

550
00:19:16,720 --> 00:19:18,600
The first 911 call reporting a fire

551
00:19:18,600 --> 00:19:20,691
came in less than forty
seconds after ignition.

552
00:19:21,300 --> 00:19:25,480
West Warwick police officers were already
outside the building and reported the fire

553
00:19:25,480 --> 00:19:26,743
about a minute after ignition.

554
00:19:27,220 --> 00:19:29,540
That brought a dispatch of four engine

555
00:19:29,540 --> 00:19:31,224
companies, a tower-ladder truck,

556
00:19:31,640 --> 00:19:33,535
a rescue unit and a battalion chief.

557
00:19:34,280 --> 00:19:36,480
The first apparatus were sitting about five

558
00:19:36,480 --> 00:19:38,640
hundred metres west on the same avenue,

559
00:19:38,640 --> 00:19:42,340
and were on scene within three
minutes of dispatch - roughly five

560
00:19:42,340 --> 00:19:44,267
and a half minutes into the fire.

561
00:19:44,540 --> 00:19:45,560
And there is a detail

562
00:19:45,560 --> 00:19:47,680
in the staffing that says something

563
00:19:47,680 --> 00:19:50,360
about small-town American
fire cover in 2003.

564
00:19:51,260 --> 00:19:55,600
The first engine carried one firefighter
and one fire officer. Two people.

565
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:57,980
Water was flowing from that engine's own

566
00:19:57,980 --> 00:19:59,348
tank at about six minutes.

567
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:01,940
The battalion chief invoked a mutual aid

568
00:20:01,940 --> 00:20:04,180
agreement, which brought seven more engine

569
00:20:04,180 --> 00:20:06,180
and ladder companies from surrounding towns,

570
00:20:06,180 --> 00:20:08,120
and requested twelve ambulances.

571
00:20:08,710 --> 00:20:11,160
A mass casualty plan was running within

572
00:20:11,160 --> 00:20:11,980
about ten minutes

573
00:20:11,980 --> 00:20:13,559
of the first engine's arrival.

574
00:20:13,820 --> 00:20:15,560
That is a fast response by any

575
00:20:15,560 --> 00:20:17,840
measure, and NIST closes the question

576
00:20:17,840 --> 00:20:18,800
in one sentence.

577
00:20:19,680 --> 00:20:21,440
Given the hazardous mix of materials

578
00:20:21,440 --> 00:20:23,240
in The Station and the lack

579
00:20:23,240 --> 00:20:26,300
of installed sprinklers,
nothing that the fire

580
00:20:26,300 --> 00:20:28,580
department could have done that night

581
00:20:28,580 --> 00:20:30,901
would have saved the building
from the fast-growing fire.

582
00:20:31,460 --> 00:20:32,920
Read that as the technical

583
00:20:32,920 --> 00:20:33,840
statement it is.

584
00:20:34,400 --> 00:20:35,720
It is not consolation

585
00:20:35,720 --> 00:20:37,280
and it is not a compliment.

586
00:20:38,140 --> 00:20:39,040
It is a boundary.

587
00:20:39,800 --> 00:20:41,660
The event was over before the profession

588
00:20:41,660 --> 00:20:43,720
that exists to stop it

589
00:20:43,720 --> 00:20:44,878
could physically be present.

590
00:20:45,300 --> 00:20:46,700
Which is precisely why

591
00:20:46,700 --> 00:20:48,226
the sprinkler timing matters.

592
00:20:48,780 --> 00:20:53,360
The thing that would have been there in thirty-three
seconds was not a truck. It was a pipe.

593
00:20:54,140 --> 00:20:55,880
So which of those two changes

594
00:20:55,880 --> 00:20:56,880
would have mattered more?

595
00:20:57,240 --> 00:20:58,840
The report does not rank them

596
00:20:58,840 --> 00:21:00,560
and this programme will not either,

597
00:21:00,560 --> 00:21:02,500
because they are not alternatives.

598
00:21:03,100 --> 00:21:05,462
They are the same finding twice.

599
00:21:05,660 --> 00:21:08,860
One is about the time the room
gives you, and the other

600
00:21:08,860 --> 00:21:10,460
is about how fast the room can empty.

601
00:21:11,440 --> 00:21:16,120
Water at thirty-three seconds makes
the deadline later. A door twice as wide makes

602
00:21:16,120 --> 00:21:17,067
the queue shorter.

603
00:21:17,620 --> 00:21:19,673
Either one moves the two numbers apart,

604
00:21:19,860 --> 00:21:22,523
and the whole of that night
lives in the gap between them.

605
00:21:23,040 --> 00:21:24,680
One hundred people lost their lives

606
00:21:24,680 --> 00:21:25,658
in the fire.

607
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:27,947
NIST names three direct contributors,

608
00:21:28,220 --> 00:21:29,760
and it is worth hearing all three

609
00:21:29,760 --> 00:21:31,840
in order, because most tellings

610
00:21:31,840 --> 00:21:33,420
of this night stop after the first

611
00:21:33,420 --> 00:21:35,000
word of the first one.

612
00:21:35,120 --> 00:21:35,910
One:

613
00:21:35,940 --> 00:21:37,993
the hazardous mix of building contents.

614
00:21:38,240 --> 00:21:39,140
Two:

615
00:21:39,280 --> 00:21:41,860
the inadequate capability
to suppress the fire

616
00:21:41,860 --> 00:21:43,597
during its early stage of growth.

617
00:21:44,020 --> 00:21:44,920
Three:

618
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:46,920
the inability of the exits to handle

619
00:21:46,920 --> 00:21:48,640
all of the occupants in the short

620
00:21:48,640 --> 00:21:51,240
time available for such a fast-growing fire.

621
00:21:51,880 --> 00:21:52,780
Contents.

622
00:21:53,320 --> 00:21:54,220
Suppression.

623
00:21:54,520 --> 00:21:55,410
Exits.

624
00:21:55,440 --> 00:21:56,560
Not one of those three

625
00:21:56,560 --> 00:21:57,460
is a person.

626
00:21:57,700 --> 00:21:59,500
And then the sentence in the report

627
00:21:59,500 --> 00:22:01,240
that is easiest to read past

628
00:22:01,240 --> 00:22:02,398
and hardest to answer.

629
00:22:03,020 --> 00:22:04,660
Both of the 2003 editions

630
00:22:04,660 --> 00:22:08,892
of the major model building and fire codes
have provisions that address these problems.

631
00:22:09,460 --> 00:22:10,580
The codes of that year

632
00:22:10,580 --> 00:22:11,580
already said it.

633
00:22:12,140 --> 00:22:14,000
NIST's recommendations are about closing

634
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,940
the distance between what a code
says and what a building is.

635
00:22:17,580 --> 00:22:19,780
It asks that pyrotechnic devices be banned

636
00:22:19,780 --> 00:22:21,900
from indoor use in nightclubs

637
00:22:21,900 --> 00:22:23,900
that do not have a compliant
sprinkler system.

638
00:22:24,420 --> 00:22:25,940
It asks that the factor of safety

639
00:22:25,940 --> 00:22:28,380
used to set occupancy limits be increased.

640
00:22:28,960 --> 00:22:31,040
And it lists, without adjectives,

641
00:22:31,040 --> 00:22:33,560
the ordinary behaviours that quietly disable

642
00:22:33,560 --> 00:22:34,620
a protective system:

643
00:22:35,420 --> 00:22:37,930
installing decorations
that exceed the flame-spread

644
00:22:37,960 --> 00:22:40,900
provisions, exposing
a building to strong ignition

645
00:22:40,900 --> 00:22:44,140
sources, exceeding
the posted occupancy limits,

646
00:22:44,740 --> 00:22:46,220
temporarily blocking an exit,

647
00:22:46,220 --> 00:22:48,378
and disabling sprinklers for maintenance.

648
00:22:49,120 --> 00:22:51,560
Every item on that list is a thing somebody

649
00:22:51,560 --> 00:22:52,540
does on a normal Tuesday,

650
00:22:52,540 --> 00:22:54,060
for a normal reason,

651
00:22:54,060 --> 00:22:54,900
in a building

652
00:22:54,900 --> 00:22:55,980
that is not on fire.

653
00:22:56,480 --> 00:22:58,680
There is one more recommendation,

654
00:22:58,680 --> 00:23:00,140
and it is the most honest thing

655
00:23:00,140 --> 00:23:01,172
in the document.

656
00:23:01,500 --> 00:23:02,920
NIST says there is a clear need

657
00:23:02,920 --> 00:23:05,280
to understand better how people and crowds

658
00:23:05,280 --> 00:23:07,880
behave in emergencies, in order to pinpoint

659
00:23:07,880 --> 00:23:08,980
the factors that lead

660
00:23:08,980 --> 00:23:09,985
to crowd crush —

661
00:23:10,100 --> 00:23:11,640
so that buildings can be designed

662
00:23:11,640 --> 00:23:12,851
to make it less likely.

663
00:23:13,340 --> 00:23:15,077
Which is an official body saying,

664
00:23:15,280 --> 00:23:16,680
in print, that it

665
00:23:16,680 --> 00:23:18,740
does not yet know why a doorway

666
00:23:18,740 --> 00:23:20,319
does what a doorway did that night.

667
00:23:20,580 --> 00:23:22,020
It is worth knowing how careful

668
00:23:22,020 --> 00:23:26,600
the modelling was, because the four-hundred-and-twenty
figure is doing a lot of work

669
00:23:26,600 --> 00:23:27,500
in this film.

670
00:23:28,040 --> 00:23:30,100
NIST ran the evacuation through two separate

671
00:23:30,100 --> 00:23:32,580
commercial packages, and had to decide

672
00:23:32,580 --> 00:23:34,940
where to put four hundred and twenty

673
00:23:34,940 --> 00:23:36,600
people inside the floor plan

674
00:23:36,601 --> 00:23:40,020
before it could start. It assumed the dance
floor and the area around the platform

675
00:23:40,020 --> 00:23:42,440
were at the maximum density the model

676
00:23:42,440 --> 00:23:44,600
codes allow - a little over two

677
00:23:44,600 --> 00:23:45,916
people per square metre -

678
00:23:46,340 --> 00:23:47,840
and the sun room and the raised

679
00:23:47,840 --> 00:23:49,620
area around the dance floor

680
00:23:49,620 --> 00:23:50,520
at about one and a half.

681
00:23:50,780 --> 00:23:52,420
That is a crowded room,

682
00:23:52,420 --> 00:23:55,040
modelled honestly as a crowded room,

683
00:23:55,040 --> 00:23:56,540
at the number the code

684
00:23:56,540 --> 00:23:57,645
would have permitted.

685
00:23:57,940 --> 00:23:59,380
And one last thing about how

686
00:23:59,380 --> 00:24:00,643
this report was written,

687
00:24:00,680 --> 00:24:01,760
which is not a finding

688
00:24:01,760 --> 00:24:02,813
and is in it anyway.

689
00:24:03,520 --> 00:24:05,309
To understand who got out and why,

690
00:24:05,900 --> 00:24:09,361
investigators tracked individual people
frame by frame across the video -

691
00:24:09,720 --> 00:24:14,107
from where they were standing when the foam
lit, to the moment they left the building.

692
00:24:14,200 --> 00:24:15,989
One man is followed the whole way.

693
00:24:16,380 --> 00:24:19,340
The report says that his face
has been blurred in the published figures

694
00:24:19,340 --> 00:24:21,340
in order to preserve his anonymity.

695
00:24:22,060 --> 00:24:23,600
A federal technical report,

696
00:24:24,040 --> 00:24:25,820
in the middle of a fluid dynamics

697
00:24:25,820 --> 00:24:28,840
investigation, stopping
to protect a survivor

698
00:24:28,840 --> 00:24:30,020
from being recognised.

699
00:24:32,320 --> 00:24:33,531
Go back to the doorway.

700
00:24:33,920 --> 00:24:34,820
It is empty.

701
00:24:35,240 --> 00:24:36,680
The wide doors are propped,

702
00:24:36,820 --> 00:24:37,925
the vestibule is lit,

703
00:24:38,260 --> 00:24:40,160
and the single door beyond is standing

704
00:24:40,160 --> 00:24:41,560
open onto a room where a band

705
00:24:41,560 --> 00:24:42,460
is setting up.

706
00:24:42,720 --> 00:24:44,560
That is what a building looks like

707
00:24:44,560 --> 00:24:45,981
when everything is correct.

708
00:24:46,360 --> 00:24:48,900
It looks like a way in. The foam

709
00:24:48,900 --> 00:24:50,321
was on the walls for sound.

710
00:24:50,680 --> 00:24:52,200
The tables came out so more

711
00:24:52,200 --> 00:24:53,200
people could stand.

712
00:24:53,900 --> 00:24:54,980
The vestibule was there

713
00:24:54,980 --> 00:24:56,540
so February stayed outside.

714
00:24:57,460 --> 00:24:59,761
Nobody in that chain
was doing anything unusual.

715
00:25:00,400 --> 00:25:01,820
Each decision was small,

716
00:25:02,160 --> 00:25:04,660
sensible, and about something other

717
00:25:04,660 --> 00:25:06,720
than the night it turned out to be about.

718
00:25:07,040 --> 00:25:09,060
And the arithmetic underneath all of them

719
00:25:09,060 --> 00:25:11,180
was the same arithmetic the whole time:

720
00:25:11,800 --> 00:25:13,340
a room holds as many people

721
00:25:13,340 --> 00:25:14,720
as it can put back outside,

722
00:25:14,720 --> 00:25:16,660
in the time the room gives them.

723
00:25:17,420 --> 00:25:18,320
Ninety seconds.

724
00:25:18,360 --> 00:25:19,260
One door.

725
00:25:19,600 --> 00:25:21,040
Between a half and two thirds

726
00:25:21,040 --> 00:25:22,510
of everyone in the building,

727
00:25:22,540 --> 00:25:24,435
walking toward the way they came in.

728
00:25:25,020 --> 00:25:26,920
Tonight, in a small venue somewhere,

729
00:25:27,480 --> 00:25:28,700
a fire officer is standing

730
00:25:28,700 --> 00:25:30,360
in a vestibule with a tape measure

731
00:25:30,360 --> 00:25:32,460
and a clipboard, working out a number.

732
00:25:33,100 --> 00:25:34,837
The number is not about the room.

733
00:25:35,340 --> 00:25:36,445
It is about the door.
